Yes, you can determine if a Slack message has been read by its ID using the Slack API. The `message.read` event is triggered when a user reads a message in a channel or direct message. You can use this event to track whether a message has been read or not.

To do this, you can use the Slack Events API to subscribe to the `message.read` event. When you receive an event with a `type` of `message.read`, you can check the `message` object to see if it matches the message you're interested in. If it does, you can mark the message as read in your system.

Here's an example of how you might do this using the Slack Events API Node.js library:
```javascript
const { WebClient } = require('@slack/web-client');

const webClient = new WebClient(process.env.SLACK_API_KEY);

async function handleMessageReadEvent(event) {
  const message = event.message;
  if (message.ts === 'your_message_ts') {
    // Mark the message as read in your system
    console.log('Message read:', message.ts);
  }
}

webClient.on('message.read', handleMessageReadEvent);
```
Replace `'your_message_ts'` with the timestamp of the message you want to track. This will only trigger the event when the message is read, and you can use it to mark the message as read in your system.

