To fix the CORS error in WordPress when using Next.js, you can add the following code to your `next.config.js` file:

```javascript
module.exports = {
  async headers() {
    return [
      {
        source: '/api/:path*',
        headers: [
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials', value: 'true' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin', value: 'https://your-wordpress-site.com' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Methods', value: 'GET,OPTIONS,PATCH,DELETE,POST' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Headers', value: 'X-CSRF-Token, X-Requested-With, Accept, Accept-Version, Content-Length, Content-MD5, Content-Type, Date, X-Api-Version' },
        ],
      },
    ];
  },
};
```

Replace `https://your-wordpress-site.com` with the URL of your WordPress site. This will allow Next.js to make API requests to your WordPress site while handling CORS correctly.

To fix the CORS error in Next.js while using WordPress, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the `cors` package in your Next.js project by running `npm install cors`.
2. Create a new file named `cors.js` in your `pages` directory.
3. Add the following code to the `cors.js` file:
```javascript
import { useRouter } from 'next/router';
import { CORS } from 'cors';

const router = useRouter();

const cors = new CORS({
  methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE'],
  allowedHeaders: ['Content-Type', 'Authorization'],
  origin: (origin, callback) => {
    if (origin === window.location.origin || origin === 'https://your-wordpress-site.com') {
      callback(null, true);
    } else {
      callback(new Error('Invalid origin'), false);
    }
  },
});

export default cors.createHandler({
  async handle(req, res) {
    await cors.handle(req, res);
  },
});
```
Replace `'https://your-wordpress-site.com'` with the actual URL of your WordPress site.
4. In your `next.config.js` file, add the following code to enable the `cors.js` file:
```javascript
module.exports = {
  // ...
  page: {
    async __afterRender(pageContext) {
      const { req } = pageContext;
      if (req && req.headers.host !== 'localhost') {
        await import('./pages/cors');
      }
    },
  },
};
```
This will ensure that the `cors.js` file is only loaded when the request is not coming from `localhost`.
