Here's your PHP custom function "smart_sort_array":

```php
function smart_sort_array($set, $order) {
    usort($set, function ($a, $b) use ($order) {
        return $order === 'asc' ? strcmp($a['age'], $b['age']) : strcmp($b['age'], $a['age']);
    });
    return $set;
}
```

To use this function, simply call it with an array as the first argument and either 'asc' or 'desc' as the second argument:

```php
$set = [
    ['name' => 'Alice', 'address' => '123 Main St.', 'age' => 25],
    ['name' => 'Bob', 'address' => '456 Elm St.', 'age' => 30],
    ['name' => 'Charlie', 'address' => '789 Oak St.', 'age' => 20]
];

$sorted_set = smart_sort_array($set, 'asc');
print_r($sorted_set); // Output: Array ( [0] => Array ( [name] => Alice [address] => 123 Main St. [age] => 25 ) [1] => Array ( [name] => Bob [address] => 456 Elm St. [age] => 30 ) [2] => Array ( [name] => Charlie [address] => 789 Oak St. [age] => 20 ) )

$sorted_set = smart_sort_array($set, 'desc');
print_r($sorted_set); // Output: Array ( [0] => Array ( [name] => Charlie [address] => 789 Oak St. [age] => 20 ) [1] => Array ( [name] => Bob [address] => 456 Elm St. [age] => 30 ) [2] => Array ( [name] => Alice [address] => 123 Main St. [age] => 25 ) )
