</body>
</html>
```
This code will display a yellow sun during the day (between 6 AM and 6 PM) and a red and orange sun during sunset (between 6 PM and 10 PM). You can adjust the CSS styles and the PHP condition to customize the appearance of the sun and the transition between the different states

When you add an attribute to a model in MVC, it affects both the model and the view. Here's how:

1. Model: The model represents the data and business logic of your application. When you add an attribute to a model, you're essentially adding a new property or field to that model. This new attribute can be used to store additional information about the data. For example, if you have a model for a customer, you might add an attribute called "phone number" to store the customer's phone number.
2. View: The view is responsible for displaying the data from the model to the user. When you add an attribute to a model, you need to update the view to reflect the new attribute. This can involve adding a new element to the view, such as a text input field or a label, to display the new attribute. For example, if you add a "phone number" attribute to a customer model, you might add a text input field to the view where the user can enter their phone number.

Here's an example of how this might look in code:

```csharp
// Model
public class Customer
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public string Email { get; set; }
    public string PhoneNumber { get; set; }
}

// View
@model MyProject.Models.Customer

<form>
    <label for="name">Name:</label>
    <input type="text" id="name" name="name" value="@Model.Name" />

    <label for="email">Email:</label>
    <input type="email" id="email" name="email" value="@Model.Email" />

    <label for="phone-number">Phone Number:</label>
    <input type="text" id="phone-number" name="phone-number" value="@Model.PhoneNumber" />
</form>
```

In this example, we have a `Customer` model with three attributes: `Name`, `Email`, and `PhoneNumber`. We also have a view that displays a form for creating or editing a customer. The view uses the `@model` directive to specify the model type, and then uses the `@Model` object to access the model's properties.

