To pass credentials in JS code for `react-native-fbsdk-next`, you can use the following approach:

1. Create a separate file (e.g., `credentials.js`) to store your Facebook SDK credentials.
2. Export the credentials object from the `credentials.js` file.
3. Import the credentials object into your React Native component and use it to initialize the Facebook SDK.

Here's an example of how you can implement this:

```javascript
// credentials.js
const facebookSDKCredentials = {
  appId: 'your_app_id',
  apiKey: 'your_api_key',
  pageId: 'your_page_id',
};

export default facebookSDKCredentials;
```

```javascript
// App.js (or any other component where you want to use the Facebook SDK)
import React, { useEffect } from 'react';
import { View, Text } from 'react-native';
import { FacebookSDK } from 'react-native-fbsdk-next';
import credentials from './credentials';

FacebookSDK.initializeApp(credentials);

const App = () => {
  useEffect(() => {
    // Your app initialization code here
  }, []);

  return (
    <View style={{ flex: 1, justifyContent: 'center', alignItems: 'center' }}>
      <Text>Hello, World!</Text>
    </View>
  );
};

export default App;
```

In this example, we first create a `credentials.js` file that exports an object containing our Facebook SDK credentials. We then import the credentials object into our `App.js` component and use it to initialize the Facebook SDK using the `FacebookSDK.initializeApp()` method.

This way, you can easily pass your Facebook SDK credentials in JS code for `react-native-fbsdk-next`. Make sure to replace the placeholders with your actual Facebook SDK credentials.

To pass credentials in JS code for `react-native-fbsdk-next`, you can use the `credentials` object in the login function. Here's an example:

```javascript
import { LoginButton } from 'react-native-fbsdk-next';

const credentials = {
  email: 'your_email@example.com',
  password: 'your_password',
};

const handleLogin = async () => {
  try {
    await LoginButton.loginWithCredentials(credentials);
    console.log('Logged in successfully');
