Yes, when the server sets `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` to `\*`, it allows requests from any origin, including those without proper authentication. This means that your request might not include the authorization header, as the browser may not send it if it believes the request is allowed from any origin.

To include the authorization header in your request, you can use a more restrictive `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` value, such as the specific domain or IP address of your application. This will ensure that only requests from authorized sources can include the authorization header.

Alternatively, you can use a preflight request (OPTIONS method) to check if the server supports the authorization header before sending the actual request. If the server supports the authorization header, you can include it in the subsequent requests.

Here's an example of how you can use a preflight request in JavaScript:
```javascript
fetch('https://example.com', {
  method: 'OPTIONS',
  headers: {
    'Authorization': 'Bearer your_access_token',
    'Content-Type': 'application/json'
  }
})
  .then(response => response.ok)
  .then(response => fetch('https://example.com', {
    method: 'GET',
    headers: {
      'Authorization': 'Bearer your_access_token',
      'Content-Type': 'application/json'
    }
  }))
  .then(response => response.json())
  .catch(error => console.error(error));
```
In this example, we first send a preflight request to `https://example.com` with the `OPTIONS` method and the necessary headers, including the authorization header. If the server supports the authorization header, we proceed with sending the actual request using the `GET` method.

