Here's a simple React utility that removes widows from text. It uses the `wordWrap` property to wrap words at the end of a line and the `maxWidth` property to set the maximum width of each line.
```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text, maxWidth }) => {
  const [wrappedText, setWrappedText] = useState('');

  const handleChange = (event) => {
    const newText = event.target.value;
    setWrappedText(newText);
  };

  const removeWidows = () => {
    const lines = wrappedText.split('\n');
    const newLines = [];

    for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
      const line = lines[i];
      const words = line.split(' ');
      const newLine = words.map((word) => word.trim()).join(' ') + '\n';
      newLines.push(newLine);
    }

    setWrappedText(newLines.join(''));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <textarea value={wrappedText} onChange={handleChange} />
      <button onClick={removeWidows}>Remove Widows</button>
      <pre>{wrappedText}</pre>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```
To use this utility, simply pass in the text you want to remove widows from and the maximum width you want to set for each line. The `handleChange` function will update the `wrappedText` state when the text is changed, and the `removeWidows` function will remove the widows from the text and update the `wrappedText` state. The `pre` element will display the wrapped text with the widows removed.

Here's a simple React utility that removes widows (words or phrases that appear at the end of a line without proper justification) from a given text. This utility uses the `wordWrap` and `lineClamp` CSS properties to achieve this.

```javascript
import React, { useRef } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text }) => {
  const textRef = useRef(null);

  const handleResize = () => {
    const textNode = textRef.current;
    if (textNode) {
      const width = textNode.offsetWidth;
      const lines = textNode.textContent.split('\n');

      for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
        const line = lines[i];
        const words = line.split(' ');

        for (let j = 0; j < words.length; j++) {
          const word = words[j];
          const wordWidth = textNode.offsetWidth - 10; // Adjust the padding as needed

          if (wordWidth < word.length * 8) { // Assuming each character takes up 8 pixels in width
            words.splice(j, 1);
            j--;
          }
        }

        lines[i] = words.join(' ');
      }

      textNode.textContent = lines.join('\n');
    }
  };

  return (
    <div ref={textRef} onResize={handleResize}>
      {text}
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this component, simply pass in the text you want to remove widows from:

