To achieve this task, you can use PowerShell along with the ActiveDirectory module. First, make sure you have installed the ActiveDirectory module by running the following command:

```powershell
Install-Module -Name ActiveDirectory
```

Now, create a PowerShell script to check for ACLs that reference SIDs present in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain. Save the below code into a file named `CheckSIDHistory.ps1`.

```powershell
# Import the ActiveDirectory module
Import-Module ActiveDirectory

# Function to check if a given SID exists in SIDHistory
Function CheckSIDHistory($sid) {
    $domain = (Get-ADDomain).Name
    $sidHistory = Get-ADUser -Filter "SIDHistory -eq '$sid'" -Properties SIDHistory
    return ($sidHistory -ne $null)
}

# Function to check ACLs of a folder and its subfolders
Function CheckFolderACLs($folderPath) {
    Get-Acl -Path $folderPath | ForEach-Object {
        $accessRules = $_.Access | Where-Object { $_.IdentityReference -match '^S-1-5-21-' }
        foreach ($accessRule in $accessRules) {
            $sid = New-Object System.Security.Principal.SecurityIdentifier($accessRule.IdentityReference)
            if (CheckSIDHistory($sid.Value)) {
                Write-Host "SID $sid.Value found in SIDHistory"
            }
        }
    }

    # Recursively check subfolders
    Get-ChildItem -Path $folderPath -Directory | ForEach-Object {
        CheckFolderACLs($_)
    }
}

# Example usage
$folderPath = "C:\Path\To\Your\Folder"
CheckFolderACLs($folderPath)
```

Replace `C:\Path\To\Your\Folder` with the path you want to start checking from. This script will search for ACLs containing SIDs present in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain, starting at the specified folder and traversing through its subfolders.

Here's a PowerShell script that recursively checks folders for ACLs referring to SIDs found only in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain:

```powershell
# Define the domain name
$domain = "your_domain_name"

# Define the folder path to start the recursion
$folderPath = "C:\Your\Folder\Path"

# Function to check if a SID is present only in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain
function CheckSIDInSIDHistory($sid) {
    $domainUsers = Get-ADUser -Filter {Domain -eq $domain}
    foreach ($user in $domainUsers) {
        if (($user.SID -eq $sid) -or ($user.SIDHistory -contains $sid)) {
            return $true
        }
    }
    $domainGroups = Get-ADGroup -Filter {Domain -eq $domain}
    foreach ($group in $domainGroups) {
        if (($group.SID -eq $sid) -or ($group.SIDHistory -contains $sid)) {
            return $true
        }
    }
    return $false
}

# Recursively check the folder and its subfolders for ACLs with SIDs found only in the SIDHistory
function CheckFolderACLs($folderPath) {
    Get-ChildItem -Path $folderPath -Directory | ForEach-Object {
        $acl = Get-Acl -Path $_.FullName
        foreach ($access in $acl.Access) {
            if ($access.IdentityReference -match '^S-1-5-21-') {
                $sid = New-Object System.Security.Principal.SecurityIdentifier($access.IdentityReference)
                if (CheckSIDInSIDHistory($sid)) {
                    Write-Host "ACL in $($_.FullName) refers to a SID ($($sid.Value)) found only in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain."
                }
            }
        }
        CheckFolderACLs($_.FullName)
    }
}

# Call the function to check the folder and its subfolders
CheckFolderACLs($

```powershell
# Define function to check if an SID is present in SIDHistory
function CheckSIDInSIDHistory($sid) {
    $domain = (Get-ADDomain).Name
    $sidHistory = Get-ADUser -Filter "SID -eq '$sid'" -Properties SIDHistory | Select-Object -ExpandProperty SIDHistory
    return ($sidHistory -ne $null)
}

# Define function to recursively check folder permissions
function CheckFolderPermissions($folderPath) {
    Get-Acl -Path $folderPath -Recurse | ForEach-Object {
        foreach ($access in $_.Access) {
            if ($access.IdentityReference -match '^S-1-5-21-') {
                $sid = New-Object System.Security.Principal.SecurityIdentifier($access.IdentityReference)
                if (CheckSIDInSIDHistory($sid)) {
                    Write-Host "Found SID in SIDHistory: $($access.IdentityReference)"
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

# Example usage
$folderPath = "C:\YourFolderPath"
CheckFolderPermissions($folderPath)
```

Replace `"C:\YourFolderPath"` with the path you want to start checking from. This script will recurse through the specified folder and its subfolders, checking for any ACLs that reference SIDs found in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain.
